\id XXC
\mt1 Textual Variants
\p The New Testament of this edition is the King James Version conformed to the Byzantine text of the Greek New Testament, as edited by Maurice A. Robinson and William G. Pierpont (2026).
\p The King James translators worked from the Received Text: the Greek printed by Erasmus, Stephanus and Beza, as F. H. A. Scrivener reconstructed it from their choices in 1894. The two Greek texts differ in some nineteen hundred places.
\p Where English can show the difference, the King James words are changed, as little as the Greek requires and in the translators’ own English, and a note gives the words they had. Where English cannot show it, nothing is changed.
\p The note is marked TR where the apparatuses of Robinson and Pierpont and of Boyd show Scrivener and Stephanus agreeing, and KJV otherwise.
\p A few changes concern whole verses. Four verses the Byzantine manuscripts lack are omitted, each quoted in a note on the verse before it. The doxology of Romans stands at {ROM 14:24-26}, where those manuscripts have it. And {MAT 23:13} and {MAT 23:14} exchange places.
\p The changes start from William G. Pierpont’s Some Improvements to the King James Version from the Majority Greek Manuscripts (1990). Where an instruction cannot be applied or does not fit, a parallel in the King James Version is preferred, then Pierpont’s own wording, then the Revised Version of 1881, then Robert Adam Boyd’s American Standard Version, Byzantine Text (2021).
\p Boyd’s Text-Critical English New Testament (2022; © 2021 Robert Adam Boyd; CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/; source: https://archive.org/details/TCENT) and Graham G. Thomason’s Far Above All are occasionally consulted, in that order. Borrowed wording is adapted to the English of the King James Version.
\p Also consulted were Robinson’s Greek texts and his collation of Scrivener with them, Boyd’s Text-Critical Greek New Testament (2022), the Majority Standard Bible, the World English Bible, the American Standard Version of 1901, and the CrossWire Bible Society’s tagging of the King James Version.
\p The variants list every verse in which the two Greek texts differ, or in which this edition corrects the King James English where the Greek agrees. Entries run book by book under this edition’s verse numbers, with the King James number beside any that differs.
\p Each entry compares the Received Text (TR) with the Byzantine text (RP26), and, where the English changes, the King James Version (KJV) with this edition (OLEB). Where the apparatuses do not show Stephanus agreeing with Scrivener, the Greek is labelled Scrivener’s.
\p An ellipsis marks words left out; a verse that cannot be shortened is quoted whole. The verses the Byzantine text lacks are given in full where the King James Bible has them.
\p The Greek is Scrivener’s and the main text of RP26, with their accents and punctuation; where an apparatus reads otherwise, its reading is labelled. Both English texts follow this edition’s spelling and punctuation, without the revisions made to single verses.
